        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The third NASA-Mir mission, NASA 3, began as STS-79 launched on September 16, 1996. On the Shuttle was John Blaha, who replaced the NASA 2 long-duration U.S. crewmember, Shannon Lucid, on board Mir. Dr. Lucid took John Blaha's place on the Shuttle for the return trip to Earth.  Blaha remained on board Mir with the Mir 22 crew, Commander Valeri Korzun and Flight Engineer Alexander Kaleri, until his return to Earth in January, 1997, on STS-81.  <P>During the five days of joint operations, astronauts and cosmonauts transferred science samples and data collected by the Mir 21/NASA 2 crew from the Mir to the Shuttle. After returning to Earth, the data was analyzed by researchers. Crewmembers also transferred hardware and supplies to Mir in support of the health and well being of the Mir 22/NASA 3 crew, as well as to support future investigations on the station. Many of the resupply science items were used directly for scientific investigations performed during the NASA 3 increment.<P>As scientists learn more about the effects of the space environment, they continue to develop questions from the fields of human life sciences, fundamental biology, biotechnology, materials sciences, and spacecraft structural dynamics and environment. Valuable scientific information regarding these subjects was returned from the Shuttle-Mir science program in the disciplines of advanced technology, Earth sciences, fundamental biology, human life sciences, International Space Station risk mitigation, and microgravity sciences.<P>The commercially initiated research of the advanced technology discipline evaluated new technologies and techniques using the Mir space station and the Shuttle as unique testing environments. A better understanding of the characteristics of superconductors, protein crystal growth, and the development of biological and chemical systems through fluid processing in reduced gravity will lead to an enhanced technology base for the design and operations of the International Space Station. The knowledge gained also has the potential to significantly improve aspects of commercial processing here on Earth.<P>Earth sciences research in ocean biochemistry, land surface hydrology, and meteorology was also performed.  Observation and documentation of transient natural and human-induced changes were accomplished with the use of hand-held cameras. Earth orbit allows documentation of atmospheric conditions, ecological changes, seasonal changes over long periods and ephemeral events.<P>Fundamental biology research continued investigations that studied the effects of the space environment on the biological systems of plants. Prolonged exposure to microgravity provided an ideal opportunity to determine the role gravity plays in cell regulation and how low gravity affects development and growth. Investigations under this discipline also characterized the radiation environment inside the Mir station.<P>Human life sciences investigations focused on the crewmembers' adaptation to weightlessness in terms of skeletal muscle and bone changes, coordination and balance, psychological interactions, immune system function, and metabolism. In addition, environmental factors were assessed, including air and water quality, and surface and crew microbiology. This set of investigations continued the characterization of the integrated human response to a prolonged presence in space.<P>The International Space Station risk mitigation experiments consisted of several technology demonstrations associated with human factors and maintenance of crew health and safety aboard the space station. To improve the design and operation of the International Space Station, information to evaluate the Mir interior and exterior environments was gathered. This included investigations of radio interference, particle impact on the station, docked configuration stability, water microbiological monitoring and inventory management.<P>Microgravity research had the general goal of advancing scientific understanding through research in biotechnology, fluid physics, combustion science and materials science. The ambient acceleration and vibration environment of Mir was characterized to support future research programs.<P>The variety of science coupled with the ability to conduct investigations over long periods in microgravity, make the Shuttle-Mir missions unique and extremely valuable. Nearly all Shuttle-Mir investigations are joint efforts between the U.S. and Russia, with at least one researcher involved from each country. The knowledge gained will assist researchers in developing future space stations and operational procedures, and will advance the knowledge base of an array of science areas that will benefit all people on Earth.<END>
